('oE8j6nm','g6','1. g6 $1 ({Thematic WT} 1. bxa3 $143 hxg5 2. a4 g4 3. a5 g3 4. a6 g2 5. a7 g1=Q 6. a8=Q Qg8+ 7. Kb7 Qxa8+ 8. Kxa8 Kc6 9. Ka7 Kb5 10. a4+ Kxa4 11. Kb6 Kxb4 12. Kc6 Kc4 13. Kd6 Kd4 14. Ke6 Ke4 15. Kf6 Kf4 16. Kg7 h5 $18) hxg6 2. bxa3 g5 3. a4 g4 4. a5 g3 5. a6 g2 6. a7 g1=Q 7. a8=Q Qg8+ 8. Kb7 $1 (8. Ka7 $143 Qxa2+ $19) Qxa8+ 9. Kxa8 Kc6 (h5 $144 10. Ka7 h4 11. b5 $11) 10. Ka7 $1 (10. a4 $143 Kb6 11. a5+ Ka6 12. Kb8 h5 13. Kc7 h4 14. Kc6 h3 15. b5+ Kxa5 16. b6 h2 17. b7 h1=Q+ $19) Kb5 11. a4+ $1 Kxa4 (Kxb4 $144 12. Kb6 $11) 12. Kb6 $1 Kxb4 13. Kc6 Kc4 14. Kd6 Kd4 15. Ke6 Ke4 16. Kf6 Kf4 17. Kg6 $11 {draw. Done in good time effect in a P-study. The purpose of the 1st move becomes apparent only by the end. Despite the fact that ,in the main line, after the 10th move, Reti-manoeuvre is well-known, the synthesis is very good.}'),
('yLZzpFj','d7','{Three passed pawns in the centre and on the both flanks look very dangerous. Can a sickly black knight do anything against such power?} 1. d7 Na7 2. d8=Q $1 (2. h6 $143 Nc6+ 3. Kb5 (3. Kb6 $144 Kd6 $19) Kd6 4. d8=Q+ Nxd8 5. a7 Nc6 $1 6. a8=Q Rb4+ 7. Ka6 Kc7 $1 $19) Nc6+ 3. Kb5 Nxd8 4. a7 Rb4+ $1 (Kxf5 $144 5. a8=Q Ne6 6. Qg8 Nc7+ 7. Kc5 Ne8 8. h6 Nxf6 9. h7 $18) 5. Kxb4 Nc6+ 6. Kc4 $3 ({Logical (thematic) try} 6. Kc5 $143 Nxa7 7. h6 Kxf6 {White in zz 1. The zugzwang is quite unexpected. Not only due to material advantage that promises to last long as the knight has enough freedom to escape his aggressor. The main surprise comes from a pair of pawns: g5 and f2. They are placed at the distance of one move! A spare tempo that usually helps winning battles for zugzwangs has no effect here because the pawns don''t oppose each other. The white pawn is in duel against theblackknight(correspond ing squares f2-a7, f3-c8)! And the black pawn fights vs. king (corresponding squares g5-c5, g4-d7) ! Another unusual thing here - squares c5 and d7 are neighboring! The white king jumps from one corresponding square to another like a knight!} 8. Kb6 (8. f3 $143 Nc8 $19 {zz 2.}) Nc8+ 9. Kc7 Na7 $1 10. Kd7 g4 $1 {(zz 3.)} 11. Ke8 Nc6 12. Kf8 Ne5 13. h7 Kxf5 14. Kg7 (14. h8=Q $143 Ng6+ $19 {Third fork!}) Ng6 $11) Nxa7 7. h6 Kxf6 8. Kc5 {Black in zz 1!} g4 ({main line II} Nc8 $144 9. f3 $1 {(zz 2.)} Na7 10. Kb6 Nc8+ 11. Kc7 Ne7 (Na7 $144 12. Kd7 $18 {bonus zz, not seen in the logical try.}) 12. h7 Nd5+ (Kg7 $144 13. f6+ $1 $18) 13. Kd6 $1 (13. Kc6 $143 Nb4+ 14. Kb5 Kg7 $11) Kg7 14. Kxd5 Kxh7 15. Kd6 $1 $18 (15. Ke5 $143 Kg7 $11)) 9. Kb6 Nc8+ 10. Kc7 {with: main line A} Na7 ({main line B} Ne7 $144 11. h7 Nd5+ 12. Kd6 $1 Kg7 13. Kxd5 Kxh7 {Similar pawn ending, but a pair of pawns is one rank higher. The white king corrects his move.} 14. Ke5 $1 (14. Kd6 $143 Kh6 $11) Kg7 15. Kf4 $18 {win.}) 11. Kd7 {(zz 3.)} Nb5 12. Ke8 Nd4 13. Kf8 Kxf5 14. h7 Ne6+ 15. Kg8 $18 {(e8) win. ---------------- Multivariate study of contemporary style.}'),
('L4WyCGA','f7','1. f7+ Kg7 2. h6+ (2. Bd4+ $143 Be5 3. h6+ Kxh6 (Kh8 $143 4. f8=Q+ Qxf8+ 5. Kxf8 Bxd4 6. c4 $18) 4. f8=Q+ Qxf8+ 5. Kxf8 Bxd4 6. c4 Bb6 7. Kf7 Ba5 8. c5 Bc7 9. c6 Ba5 10. Ke7 Kg7 $11) Kxh6 3. f8=Q+ Qxf8+ 4. Kxf8 Bg1 (Bg3 $144 5. Bd4 Be5 6. Ke7 $18) 5. Bg3 (5. Be1 $143 Bf2 6. Bd2 Be3 $11 {and the wB cannot escape}) (5. a5 $143 Bxf2 6. a6 Bc5+ 7. Kf7 Ba7 8. c4 Bc5 9. Kf6 (9. Ke6 $144 Kg7 10. Kd5 Ba7 11. Kc6 (11. c5 $143 Kf7 12. c6 Ke7 $19) h6 $11) Ba7 10. Kf7 Bc5 $11) Bh2 (Bf2 $144 6. Bd6 Bc5 7. Be7 $18) 6. Be1 Bg3 7. Bd2 Be1 (Bf4 $144 8. Bxf4 $18) 8. Be3 (8. Bc1 $143 Bxc3 9. Ke8 Bb2 (Kg7 $143 10. Bxg5 $18) 10. Bd2 Bc3 11. Be3 Bd4 12. Bxd4 $11 {stalemate}) Bf2 (Bxc3 $144 9. Bc5 Bb4 10. Be7 $18) 9. Bc1 Be3 10. Bb2 Bc1 11. Ba1 (11. a5 $143 Bxb2 12. a6 Ba3+ 13. Ke8 Bc5 $11) Bb2 12. a5 Bxa1 13. a6 Bxc3 14. a7 $18'),
('SeZOJH1','Rf7','1. Rf7 (1. Rxg7 $143 Bb1 2. Rg8+ Kc7 3. Rg7+ Kc6 4. Rg8 Bxe5 5. Rc8+ Bc7 $19) Bb1 2. Rf8+ Kd7 3. Rf7+ Kc6 4. Rf8 Kb7 (Bxe5 $143 5. Rf1 $18) 5. Rf7+ Kb6 6. a5+ Kb5 7. Rc7 Bxe5 8. Rc8 Bd4 9. g5 (9. a6 $143 e5 10. Kb3 Bc5 11. Ra5+ Kxa5 12. Rxc5+ Kb6 13. Rc3 e4 14. Kc4 e3 15. Kd3 c1=Q+ $19) g6 (e5 $144 10. b4 Bxa1 11. Rc5+ Ka6 12. Rc7 Kb5 13. Rc5+ Ka6 14. Rc7 $11 {positionnal draw}) 10. a6 (10. Rc7 $143 Bc5+ 11. Rxc5+ Kxc5 12. b4+ Kc4 13. Kb2 Kxb4 14. a6 e5 15. Ra3 c1=Q+ $19) Bc5+ 11. Rxc5+ Kxc5 12. b4+ Kc4 13. Kb2 Kxb4 14. Kc1 e5 15. Kd2 e4 16. Kc1 e3 17. Ra3 Kxa3 $11 {stalemate A juicy study with two rooks dealing with tow black bishops plus an advanced pawn [HH]}'),
('MdFGbcj','Na5','{If the knight didn''t interfere with his queen Pg2 would have been pinned and White could mate in a couple of moves. Consequently, the knight is allowed to do whatever he wants to. The capture of black Pd6 is a nice tactical move, but it is only a logical (thematic) try.} 1. Na5 $3 {Quiet transfer of the knight to e3.} ({Logical (thematic) try} 1. Nxd6 $143 Kh2 2. Qf3 Nf2 3. Qg3+ Kh1 4. Qf3 Kh2 5. Nc4 g1=Q 6. Nxe3 d6 $1 {White in zz!} 7. Nd1 $1 Qxd1 8. Qxf2+ Kh1 9. Kh3 Qb3+ 10. e3 Qe6+ 11. Kg3 Qe5+ $1 $11 {draw.}) Kh2 ({Black can try to get rid of his pawn} d5 $144 2. Qxd5 Kh2 3. Qxd1 {but his knight falls too} (3. Qf3 $143 Nf2 4. Nc4 g1=Q 5. Nxe3 d6 $11 {zz}) g1=Q 4. Qxg1+ Kxg1 5. Kg3 $1 $18) 2. Qf3 Nf2 3. Nc4 $1 (3. Qf4+ $143 Kh1 4. Qf3 Kh2 5. Nc4 $18 {loss of time}) g1=Q 4. Nxe3 {Black in zz! His spared Pd6 blocks the square. King, queen and knight are totally paralyzed. And what is wrong withthepawnsmoves?} e5 (d5 $144 5. Nd1 $1 Qg2 6. Qxf2 d4 7. Qf4+ Kh1 8. Nf2+ Kg1 9. Nh3+ Kh1 10. Qxd4 $18) 5. Nd1 $1 Qxd1 ({The knight cannot desert his post.} Nxd1 $144 6. Qh3#) 6. Qxf2+ Kh1 7. Kh3 (7. Kg3 $143 Qg1+ $19) Qb3+ 8. e3 Qe6+ 9. Kg3 $1 $18 {win. Black cannot play Qe5+. An evil chance seldom comes alone - because of the blocking on d6 another pawn has blocked square e5. This combination of material and tempo logical ideas with a deep logical try was called as "Krikheli forsight effect" by Andrey Visokosov. "I dedicate my study to him for his development of this interesting and complex theme in his creative work."(S.Didukh)}'),
('0ceW8Ys','Ng5','1. Ng5+ $1 ({try} 1. Ra7 $143 Bxf3+ 2. e4 Bxe4+ 3. Kxe4 b1=Q+ 4. Kd4 Nf5+ $11 {etc}) fxg5 2. Ra7 $1 (2. Rf1 $143 Bf3+ 3. Rxf3 b1=Q 4. b8=Q Qd1+ $11) Bf3+ 3. e4 $1 ({Thematic try} 3. Ke6 $143 Bxb7 4. Rxb7+ Nf7 5. Rxf7+ Kh6 6. Rf1 c2 $19) Bxe4+ 4. Ke6 $1 (4. Kxe4 $143 b1=Q+ 5. Kd4 Nf5+ $11) Bxb7 5. Rxb7+ Nf7 6. Rxf7+ Kh6 ({main} Kg8 $144 7. Ke7 b1=Q 8. Rf8+ Kh7 9. Be5 Kh6 10. Rh8#) 7. Rf1 c2 8. Rh1 c1=Q 9. Bg1+ Kg7 10. Bd4+ Kf8 11. Rh8#'),
('ktbA7wj','Rc2','1. Rc2 $1 ({Try} 1. Rc4 $143 Rxc4 $1 2. Nxb2+ Kb3 3. Nxc4 gxf2 4. Nd2+ Kc2 5. Nf3 Rh5 6. Kf1 Kd3 7. Kxf2 Kxe4 8. Ke2 Kf5 9. Kf2 e4 10. Nd4+ Kg4 11. Kg2 Rh3 12. Kf2 Rh2+ 13. Kf1 Rd2 14. Ke1 Ra2 15. Kd1 Kg3 16. Ke1 Kg2 17. Kd1 Kf2 18. Nf5 Ra5 19. Ne7 Kxe3 $19) g2 $1 (gxf2 $144 2. Rxb2 $1 Rxb2+ 3. Nxb2+ Kb3 4. Nd3 Kc3 5. Nxf2 $11) 2. Nxb2+ Ka3 (Rxb2 $144 3. Rxb2 g1=Q 4. Kd3 $1 Rg8 (Qc1 $144 5. Rfc2 Qf1+ 6. Kc3 $11) (Qd1+ $144 5. Kc4 $11) 5. Ra2+ Kb5 6. Rfb2+ Kc6 7. Ra6+ Kd7 8. Ra7+ Kd6 9. Ra6+ {perpetual} Kc5 $11 (Ke7 $143 10. Rb7+ Kf8 11. Ra8#)) (Kb3 $144 3. Rc1 g1=Q 4. Rxg1 Rxg1 5. Nd3 $11) 3. Nc4+ $1 (3. Kd3 $143 g1=Q 4. Nc4+ Rxc4 $1 (Ka4 $143 5. Ra2+ Kb5 6. Ra5+ Kc6 7. Rf6+ Kb7 (Kd7 $143 8. Ra7+ Ke8 9. Nd6+ Kd8 10. Rf8#) 8. Rf7+ Kb8 9. Rf8+ $11 {perpetual}) 5. Kxc4 Qb1 $1 $19) Rxc4 4. Rxg2 $1 Rxg2+ (Rxc2+ $144 5. Kf3 $1 $11) 5. Kd3 $11 {stalemate.}'),
