('pGujbrC','Bb4','1. Bb4 Rb1 (Rg1+ 2. Kf2 Rg8 (Rc1 3. c6 Rxc4 4. c7 Rc6 5. f4 e4 6. f5) 3. c6 Nd8 4. Be7 $1) 2. a3 (2. c6 $2 Rxb4 3. c5 Nxc5 4. c7 Ne4+ 5. Kg2 Nd6) a5 3. c6 axb4 4. c5 bxa3 (Nxc5 5. c7 Ne4+ 6. fxe4 Rc1 7. axb4) 5. c7 Rg1+ 6. Kh3 $1 (6. Kf2 $2 Rg8 7. Nd8 Rxd8 8. c6 Rd2+ 9. Ke3 Nd6) Rg8 (a2 7. c8=Q+ Rg8 8. Nd8 $1 Nxd8 9. Qa6) 7. Nd8 Rxd8 8. c6 $1 Rg8 9. cxb7 a2 10. b8=Q a1=Q 11. Qxg8+ Kxg8 12. c8=Q+ Kf7 13. Qd7+ Kg6 14. Qg7+ Kh5 15. Qf7+ Kxh6 16. Qxf6+ Kh5 17. Qg5#'),
('3J9SAPy','g7','{This spectacular study involves sacrifices by both White and Black, with the key idea being the move 3 c4!, opening the long diagonal ready to cover the a1-square several moves later. The whole idea is realised with great economy.} 1. g7 Re8+ $3 {The only chance is to sacrifice the rook to clear the e7-square. After} (Re5 $144 2. Bd1+ Kxc3 3. g8=Q $19 {White has too much material.}) 2. Bxe8 Ne7 3. c4 $3 {A remarkable move which has as its sole purpose the opening of the long diagonal. The immediate} (3. g8=Q $143 {leads only to a draw:} Bxg8 4. Bf7+ $1 (4. hxg8=Q+ $144 Nxg8 5. Kxg8 Kxc3 $11 {is a draw because White cannot win the h4-pawn without allowing the black king to reach h8, with a standard rook''s pawn(s) + wrong bishop draw.}) Bxf7 5. Kg7 Ng6 6. Kxf7 Nh8+ 7. Kg7 a4 8. Kxh8 a3 9. Kg8 (9. Kg7 $144 a2 10. h8=Q a1=Q 11. Qxh4 Qxc3+ $11 {draws at once}) a2 10. h8=Q a1=Q 11. Qxh4 Qa8+ {and White cannot avoid the checks without making so many concessions that Black is able to draw; for example,} 12. Kg7 Qb7+ 13. Kg6 Qc6+ 14. Qf6 Qg2+ 15. Qg5 (15. Kf7 $144 Qb7+ $11) Qxh2 $11 {EGTB 6 pièces}) Bxc4 (Kxc4 $144 4. g8=Q Bxg8 5. Bf7+ Bxf7 6. Kg7 Bd5 7. h8=Q $18 {will win in the long run. White picks up the h4-pawn and wins one of Black''s minor pieces for the front h-pawn. Black cannot then draw with a minor piece and an advanced a-pawn because White has another h-pawn.}) 4. g8=Q $1 {A neat tactic forcing the promotion of the h-pawn.} Bxg8 (Nxg8 $144 5. Kg7 $18 {wins at once.}) 5. Bf7+ $1 (5. hxg8=Q+ $143 Nxg8 6. Kxg8 Kc3 $11 {draws as in the note to White''s third move.}) Bxf7 6. Kg7 Ng6 (Bc4 $144 7. h8=Q $18 {wins as in the note to Black''s third move.}) 7. Kxf7 Nh8+ 8. Kg7 {or 8 Kg8} a4 9. Kxh8 a3 10. Kg8 a2 11. h8=Q $18 {and wins, thanks to the open long diagonal.}'),
('uBB9Dsi','e6','1. e6 (1. f5 $2 Nxf5 2. Bxe8 Nd6 3. Bc6 Nc4 4. Bf4 Be6+ 5. Kg3 Kg6) Bxe6+ 2. f5 Bxf5+ 3. exf5 Nc6 (Ng6 4. Bc6) 4. Ba4 $1 (4. Bxc6 $2 Re3+ 5. Bxe3) Re2 5. Bd1 Nd4 6. Be3 Nf3 7. f6 Rh2+ 8. Kg3 Kg6 9. Bxf3 Rh8 10. Be4+ Kf7 11. Bd4 Ke6 12. Kg4'),
('YZpTb0b','Rd1','1. Rd1 a3 $1 {To create threats along the fourth rank.} 2. bxa3 Ra4+ 3. f4 $3 {The significance of this sacrifice will become apparent only on the last move.} ({If} 3. Kg3 $2 {then} Rd4 $1 $11 {saves Black.}) Rxf4+ 4. Kg3 Rd4 $1 5. Rxd4 $1 e2 6. Rd6+ $1 Kh7 (cxd6 7. Ba5 {wins for White.}) 7. Rh6+ $1 Kxh6 8. Bg5+ $1 Kxg5 9. f4+ $1 {The point of the entire magnificent combination. There follows 10.Kf2 and White wins. This was one of the two beautiful studies composed by the legendary Dutch player especially for this event.}'),
('4nbvDU1','Rh1','1. Rh1 Rg1 $1 2. Rxg1 f2 3. Nf3 exf3 $1 4. Rg3 Kg5 5. Kd2 (5. e4 $2 Kf4 $1 6. Rxg4+ Kxg4 7. Kd2 Kf4 8. Kd3 e5 9. Bh3 Kg3) Kh4 6. Rxf3 $1 gxf3 7. e4 $1 (7. Kd3 $2 Kg3 8. Ke4 Kg4 $1 9. Bd3 Kg3 10. Bc4 Kg2 11. Kf4 f1=Q 12. Bxf1+ Kxf1 13. Kxf3 Ke1 $1) Kg4 8. Ke3 Kg3 9. Bc4 Kg2 (e5 10. Bf1) 10. e5 Kg3 11. Ke4 Kg2 12. Kf4'),
('Sm5Sbu8','Ke2','1. Ke2 Rb1 $1 (c2 2. Kxd2 Rb1 3. Kxc2 Rxa1 4. Ra8) 2. Rxb1 c2 3. Rxg6 (3. Rd1 $2 c1=Q 4. Rxd2 Qc4+) fxg6 (cxb1=Q 4. Nf4#) (Kxg6 4. Rb6+) 4. Nf4+ (4. g4+ $2 Kxh4 5. g3+ Kxg3 6. Rg1+ Bxg1 7. Kxd2 Be3+ $1 8. Kxc2 fxg4 9. Ng1 Kf2 10. d6 Kxg1 11. d7 Bg5 12. a6 g3) Kg4 5. Nh5 $3 Kxh5 (cxb1=Q 6. Nf6#) (Bd4 6. Nf6+ Bxf6 7. Rb4+) (f4 6. Nf6+ Kf5 7. g4+ Ke5 8. Nd7+ Kd6 9. Rb6+ Bxb6 10. Kxd2 Bxa5+ 11. Kxc2 Kxd7 12. Kd3) 6. g4+ $1 fxg4 7. Rh1 $1 c1=Q 8. Be1+ $1 Kg5 9. Bxd2+'),
('0gdjOvt','O-O-O','1. O-O-O (1. Rd1 cxd5 2. Rxd5+ Kc6 3. Rxc5+ Kxc5 4. f6+ e5 5. f7 Bd6 6. Rxg7 (6. Nxe5 $2 Rgf8 7. fxe8=Q Rxe8 $15) Rgf8 7. fxe8=Q Rxe8 8. Rxd7 $11) cxd5 2. Rxd5+ Kc6 3. Rxc5+ Kxc5 4. f6+ Kc6 (Kxc4 5. f7 e6 6. fxe8=Q Ba3+ 7. Kc2 Rxe8 8. Ne3#) (Kb6 5. c5+) (e5 5. f7 Bd6 (Be7 6. Rxe5+ Kd6 7. c5+ $18) (Kb6 6. Nxe5 Ba3+ 7. Kd2 Rd8 8. fxg8=Q Rxg8 9. Nxd7+) 6. Nxe5 (6. Nf6 $2 gxf6 7. fxg8=Q Rxg8 8. Rxg8 Kxc4 $11) Rgf8 (Be7 7. Rxg7 Rh8 8. fxe8=Q Rxe8 9. Rg4) 7. fxe8=Q Rxe8 8. Nf7+ Kc6 9. Rg6 Re6 10. Nd8+) 5. Rc5+ (5. f7 e6 6. Ne5+ (6. Nf6 gxf6 7. fxg8=Q Ba3+ 8. Kc2 Rxg8 9. Rxg8 $11) Kb7 7. Kc2 Be7 8. Rxg7 Rgf8 9. fxe8=Q Rxe8 10. Ng6 (10. Nxd7 Kc8) Bd6 (Bc5 11. Rxd7+ Kb6) 11. Rxd7+ Kc6 12. Rxa7 Rg8 13. Ne7+ Bxe7 14. Rxe7 Rg2+ $11) Kxc5 6. f7 g6 7. fxg8=Q Bh6+ 8. Nxh6 Rxg8 9. Nxg8'),
('vMrX411','Be5','1. Be5 $1 Qxe5 (Rxe5 2. Rf8+ Ka7 3. Nc8+ Ka8 4. Ne7+ Ka7 5. Nc6+) 2. Rf8+ Ka7 3. Nc8+ (3. Nxb5+ $2 Rxb5 4. axb5 Qe1+) Ka8 4. Nb6+ $1 (4. Ne7+ $2 Rb8) Ka7 5. Ra8+ Kxb6 6. Rxa6+ $1 Kxa6 7. axb5+ Kb6 8. Ra6+ Kxb5 9. Ra5+ Kc4 10. Rxe5 Rb5 11. Re3 $1 c5 (Kd4 12. Kd2 Rh5 13. Nc3 Rh2+ 14. Ne2+ Kc4 15. Re4+ Kb3 16. Rg4 Rf2 17. Ke3) 12. Nc3 $1 Rxb4 13. Kc2'),
